2026-11-26 02:04:48.012 | DEBUG    | agent.middlewares.summarization.memory_review:_invoke_memory_review:281 - memory review res is content='# Reconnaissance Report — `packages/core` (Koota) for the entity snapshot / rollback feature\n\n> **Important state note (read first).** The working tree is **not pristine**. `git status` shows the snapshot feature is *already partially implemented* in the tree I was asked to survey:\n>\n> ```\n>  M packages/core/src/entity/entity-methods-patch.ts\n>  M packages/core/src/entity/types.ts\n>  M packages/core/src/index.ts\n>  M packages/core/src/world/world.ts\n> ?? packages/core/src/snapshot/            (snapshot.ts, 585 lines)\n> ```\n>\n> So the report below documents **both** the pre-existing conventions (what a new module must imitate) **and** the in-tree draft of the feature (what already exists and where it deviates from the conventions). No files were modified.\n\n---\n\n## (d) Trait system — registration, id, schema, defaults, storage layout, read/write path  *(gating item)*\n\n### d.1 Trait creation — `trait()` factory, id, schema, store type\n\n`packages/core/src/trait/trait.ts:47-92`\n\n```ts\n// No reason to create a new object every time a tag trait is created.\nconst tagSchema = Object.freeze({});\nlet traitId = 0;\n\nfunction createTrait(schema?: undefined | Record<string, never>): TagTrait;\nfunction createTrait<S extends Schema>(schema: S): Trait<Norm<S>>;\nfunction createTrait<S extends Schema>(schema: S = tagSchema as S): Trait<Norm<S>> {\n    const isAoS = typeof schema === \'function\';\n    const isTag = !isAoS && Object.keys(schema).length === 0;\n    const traitType: StoreType = isAoS ? \'aos\' : isTag ? \'tag\' : \'soa\';\n\n    validateSchema(schema);\n\n    const id = traitId++;\n    const Trait = Object.assign((params: TraitValue<Norm<S>>) => [Trait, params], {\n        [$internal]: {\n            id: id,\n            set: createSetFunction[traitType](schema),\n            fastSet: createFastSetFunction[traitType](schema),\n            fastSetWithChangeDetection: createFastSetChangeFunction[traitType](schema),\n            get: createGetFunction[traitType](schema),\n            createStore: () => createStore<S>(schema),\n            relation: null,\n            type: traitType,\n        },\n    }) as Trait<Norm<S>>;\n\n    // Add public read-only properties\n    Object.defineProperty(Trait, \'id\', { value: id, writable: false, enumerable: true, configurable: false });\n    Object.defineProperty(Trait, \'schema\', { value: schema, writable: false, enumerable: true, configurable: false });\n\n    return Trait;\n}\n\nexport const trait = createTrait;\n```\n\nKey facts a **trait registry** must capture:\n\n| Fact | Evidence | Consequence for the registry |\n|---|---|---|\n| `trait.id` is a **process-global monotonic counter** (`traitId++`), not per-world | `trait.ts:49,60` | `id` is stable for the lifetime of the module instance but **not stable across processes / HMR / test isolation**. It is *not* a safe serialization key. |\n| `trait.schema` is a **public read-only property** (frozen descriptor) | `trait.ts:82-87` | Registry can read `trait.schema` directly; no need to reach into `$internal`. |\n| `trait[$internal].type` ∈ `\'tag\' \\| \'soa\' \\| \'aos\'` | `trait.ts:56,70` | Registry must record the store kind: tags have **no data**, SoA/AoS have data. |\n| `trait[$internal].relation` is `null` for plain traits, a `Relation` for relation traits | `trait.ts:69` | Registry must distinguish trait vs relation entries (a relation\'s *trait* is the storage carrier). |\n| `trait()` returns a **callable** `(params) => [Trait, params]` | `trait.ts:61` | A trait is a function object; `typeof x === \'function\'` is true for traits **and** for AoS schemas — use `isRelation` / `$internal` checks, not `typeof`. |\n\n### d.2 Per-world registration — `registerTrait`\n\n`packages/core/src/trait/trait.ts:94-125`\n\n```ts\nexport function registerTrait(world: World, trait: Trait) {\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n    const traitCtx = trait[$internal];\n\n    const data: TraitInstance = {\n        generationId: ctx.entityMasks.length - 1,\n        bitflag: ctx.bitflag,\n        trait,\n        store: traitCtx.createStore(),\n        queries: new Set(),\n        trackingQueries: new Set(),\n        notQueries: new Set(),\n        relationQueries: new Set(),\n        schema: trait.schema,\n        changeSubscriptions: new Set(),\n        addSubscriptions: new Set(),\n        removeSubscriptions: new Set(),\n    };\n\n    // Add trait to the world.\n    setTraitInstance(ctx.traitInstances, trait, data);\n    world.traits.add(trait);\n\n    // Track relations\n    if (traitCtx.relation) ctx.relations.add(traitCtx.relation);\n\n    // This ensures nested trait registrations get different bitflags.\n    incrementWorldBitflag(world);\n\n    // Setup ordered trait sync if this is an ordered trait\n    if (isOrderedTrait(trait)) setupOrderedTraitSync(world, trait);\n}\n```\n\n- Registration is **lazy**: it happens on first `addTrait` (`trait.ts:438`: `if (!hasTraitInstance(ctx.traitInstances, trait)) registerTrait(world, trait);`).\n- `TraitInstance` is stored in a **sparse array indexed by `trait.id`** — `packages/core/src/trait/trait-instance.ts:34-40`:\n  ```ts\n  export /* @inline @pure */ function hasTraitInstance(traitData: TraitInstanceArray, trait: Trait): boolean {\n      const traitId = trait.id;\n      return traitId < traitData.length && traitData[traitId] !== undefined;\n  }\n  ```\n- `world.traits` is a `Set<Trait>` of everything registered in that world (`trait.ts:115`). **This is the natural source for "all traits in this world"** if a registry is ever auto-derived, but it does *not* carry user-facing names — hence an explicit `[key, Trait]` registry is the right design.\n\n### d.3 Storage layout — SoA / AoS / tag, plain JS arrays (not typed arrays)\n\n`packages/core/src/storage/stores.ts:1-17`\n\n```ts\nexport function createStore<T extends Schema>(schema: T): Store<T>;\nexport function createStore(schema: Schema): unknown {\n    if (typeof schema === \'function\') {\n        return [];                       // AoS: one array of instances\n    } else {\n        const store: Record<string, unknown[]> = {};\n        for (const key in schema) {\n            store[key] = [];             // SoA: one plain array per property\n        }\n        return store;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/storage/types.ts:1-10`\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Storage type for trait data.\n * - AoS: Array of instances, one per entity\n * - SoA: Object with arrays, one array per property\n */\nexport type Store<T extends Schema = any> = T extends AoSFactory\n    ? ReturnType<T>[]\n    : {\n          [P in keyof T]: T[P] extends (...args: never[]) => unknown ? ReturnType<T[P]>[] : T[P][];\n      };\n```\n\n**Critical for the registry / snapshot design:**\n\n- Stores are **plain `Array`s**, *not* typed arrays. Values are stored **by reference** — objects, arrays, class instances, `BigInt` all live in the store as-is (`tests/trait.test.ts:10-17` exercises `arr: () => [...]`, `class: () => new TestClass()`, `bigInt: 1n`).\n- Therefore a snapshot **must deep-clone** trait values, otherwise the snapshot aliases live state. The in-tree draft does this with a hand-rolled `deepClone` (`snapshot.ts:73-81`) — note it does **not** preserve class prototypes or `BigInt`-safe semantics beyond primitives, and it will happily clone a `TestClass` into a plain object.\n- Indexing is **by entity id** (`getEntityId(entity)`), not by dense index — see d.5.\n- Tag traits have **no store at all** (`createStore` is never meaningfully used; `type === \'tag\'`). A snapshot must represent them as a presence marker (`true`), not `{}`.\n\n### d.4 Defaults\n\n`packages/core/src/storage/schema.ts` exports `getSchemaDefaults` (re-exported at `storage/index.ts:9`), used at `trait.ts:159-169`:\n\n```ts\nconst defaults = isOrderedTrait(trait)\n    ? getOrderedTrait(world, entity, trait)\n    : getSchemaDefaults(data.schema, traitCtx.type);\n\nif (traitCtx.type === \'aos\') {\n    setTrait(world, entity, trait, params ?? defaults, false);\n} else if (defaults) {\n    setTrait(world, entity, trait, { ...defaults, ...params }, false);\n} else if (params) {\n    setTrait(world, entity, trait, params, false);\n}\n```\n\n- Defaults are **merged shallowly** with params (`{ ...defaults, ...params }`) for SoA; AoS takes `params ?? defaults` wholesale.\n- `getSchemaDefaults` returns `undefined` for tags. So a registry entry needs `type` to know whether defaults exist at all.\n- **Rollback implication:** re-adding a trait via `addTrait` re-applies defaults, then `setTrait(..., false)` overwrites with the snapshot value. That is the correct order and is what the draft does (`snapshot.ts:321-324`).\n\n### d.5 Read / write a single trait value for one entity\n\n**Read** — `packages/core/src/trait/trait.ts:331-338, 373-381`\n\n```ts\nexport /* @inline @pure */ function getStore<C extends Trait = Trait>(world: World, trait: C): ExtractStore<C> {\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n    const instance = getTraitInstance(ctx.traitInstances, trait)!;\n    return instance.store as ExtractStore<C>;\n}\n\n/* @inline @pure */ function getTraitForTrait(world: World, entity: Entity, trait: Trait) {\n    if (!hasTrait(world, entity, trait)) return undefined;\n\n    const traitCtx = trait[$internal];\n    const store = getStore(world, trait);\n    const data = traitCtx.get(getEntityId(entity), store);\n\n    return data;\n}\n```\n\n**Write** — `packages/core/src/trait/trait.ts:406-422`\n\n```ts\n/* @inline */ function setTraitForTrait(\n    world: World, entity: Entity, trait: Trait, value: any, triggerChanged: boolean\n) {\n    const ctx = trait[$internal];\n    const store = getStore(world, trait);\n    const index = getEntityId(entity);\n\n    // A short circuit is more performance than an if statement which creates a new code statement.\n    value instanceof Function && (value = value(ctx.get(index, store)));\n\n    ctx.set(index, store, value);\n    triggerChanged && setChanged(world, entity, trait);\n}\n```\n\n**Presence check** — `packages/core/src/trait/trait.ts:319-329`\n\n```ts\nexport function hasTrait(world: World, entity: Entity, trait: Trait): boolean {\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n    const instance = getTraitInstance(ctx.traitInstances, trait);\n    if (!instance) return false;\n\n    const { generationId, bitflag } = instance;\n    const eid = getEntityId(entity);\n    const mask = ctx.entityMasks[generationId][eid];\n\n    return (mask & bitflag) === bitflag;\n}\n```\n\n**Accessor generation** — `packages/core/src/storage/accessors.ts:3-20` builds `set`/`get` via `new Function` with **hardcoded schema keys**:\n\n```ts\nfunction createSoASetFunction(schema: Schema) {\n    const keys = Object.keys(schema);\n    const setFunctionBody = keys\n        .map((key) => `if (\'${key}\' in value) store.${key}[index] = value.${key};`)\n        .join(\'\\n    \');\n    const set = new Function(\'index\', \'store\', \'value\', ...);\n```\n\nConsequences for the registry:\n\n1. **`setTrait` is a partial update for SoA** (`if (\'key\' in value)`), so writing a snapshot value that omits a key leaves the old value in place. A rollback that wants *exact* restoration must either (a) remove + re-add the trait, or (b) write a **complete** object containing every schema key. The draft writes `deepClone(value)` where `value` came from `getTrait` (which returns the full record), so it is complete — but only because `getTrait` returns all keys.\n2. **`setTrait` accepts a callback** (`value instanceof Function`). A snapshot value that is itself a function would be *invoked*. The registry/snapshot must therefore reject or wrap function-valued trait data (schema validation already forbids functions as *values* — `storage/schema.ts:33` throws on arrays/objects, and `validateSchema` is called at `trait.ts:58`).\n3. **`getTrait` returns `undefined` when the trait is absent** — snapshot code must branch on `hasTrait` first, not on the value.\n\n### d.6 Relation traits (must be in the registry too)\n\n`packages/core/src/relation/types.ts:7-14`\n\n```ts\nexport type RelationTarget = Entity | \'*\';\n\n/** A pair represents a relation + target combination */\nexport interface RelationPair<T extends Trait = Trait> {\n    readonly [$relationPair]: true;\n    [$internal]: {\n        relation: Relation<T>;\n        ...\n```\n\n- A relation is a **callable** `relation(target)` producing a `RelationPair`; its data lives in the relation\'s underlying **trait** store, addressed by `(entity, target)` rather than `(entity)`.\n- Read/write: `getRelationData` / `setRelationData` / `addRelationTarget` / `removeRelationTarget` / `getRelationTargets` (`relation/relation.ts`, imported at `snapshot.ts:6-12`).\n- **Registry must therefore hold two kinds of entries** and the snapshot format must keep them in separate maps (`traits` vs `relations`), because a relation key can never appear in `traits` and vice versa. The draft enforces this at `snapshot.ts:285-290` and `snapshot.ts:421-426`.\n\n---\n\n## (a) `packages/core/src/index.ts` — export style and where `./snapshot/index.js` belongs\n\n**Style: explicit named re-exports, one statement per module, `export type` blocks kept separate from value exports.** There is **no `export *`** anywhere in the file.\n\n`packages/core/src/index.ts:1-12` (representative)\n\n```ts\nexport { createActions } from \'./actions/create-actions\';\nexport type { Actions, ActionsInitializer, ActionRecord } from \'./actions/types\';\nexport { $internal } from \'./common\';\nexport type { Entity } from \'./entity/types\';\nexport { unpackEntity } from \'./entity/utils/pack-entity\';\nexport { createAdded } from \'./query/modifiers/added\';\nexport { createChanged } from \'./query/modifiers/changed\';\nexport { Not } from \'./query/modifiers/not\';\nexport { Or } from \'./query/modifiers/or\';\nexport { createRemoved } from \'./query/modifiers/removed\';\nexport { $modifier } from \'./query/modifier\';\nexport { createQuery, IsExcluded } from \'./query/query\';\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/index.ts:13-27` — the `export type { ... }` block convention (alphabetised, multi-line):\n\n```ts\nexport type {\n    EventType,\n    InstancesFromParameters,\n    IsNotModifier,\n    Modifier,\n    Query,\n    QueryModifier,\n    QueryParameter,\n    QueryResult,\n    QueryResultOptions,\n    QuerySubscriber,\n    QueryUnsubscriber,\n    QueryHash,\n    StoresFromParameters,\n} from \'./query/types\';\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/index.ts:47-49` — experimental APIs are annotated with a JSDoc tag on the line above:\n\n```ts\n/** @experimental This API is experimental and may change or be removed in future versions. */\nexport { ordered } from \'./relation/ordered\';\n/** @experimental This API is experimental and may change or be removed in future versions. */\nexport { OrderedList } from \'./relation/ordered-list\';\n```\n\n**Where a new `./snapshot/index.js` export belongs:** the file is ordered **alphabetically by module path** (`actions` → `common` → `entity` → `query` → `relation` → `storage` → `trait` → `universe` → `world`). `./snapshot/...` sorts between `./relation/...` and `./storage/...`, i.e. **immediately after the `relation` exports and before `storage`**. The in-tree draft currently places it at `index.ts:30-46`, which is *after* `$queryRef`/`relation` and *before* the `ordered` experimental block — consistent with that ordering.\n\n**Note on the `.js` suffix:** the existing file uses **extensionless** specifiers (`\'./actions/create-actions\'`, not `\'./actions/create-actions.js\'`). The task text says `./snapshot/index.js`; to match the codebase the export should be written **extensionless** (`from \'./snapshot\'` or `from \'./snapshot/index\'`), unless the build config requires `.js` (see (f) — `tsconfig` extends `@config/typescript`, and `package.json` has no `exports` map, so extensionless is what the rest of the package uses).\n\n**Current in-tree draft** (`index.ts:30-46`) — already follows the convention, but points at a single file rather than a directory barrel:\n\n```ts\nexport {\n    createTraitRegistry,\n    diffEntitySnapshots,\n    diffWorldSnapshots,\n    rollbackEntity,\n    rollbackWorld,\n    snapshotEntity,\n    snapshotWorld,\n} from \'./snapshot/snapshot\';\nexport type {\n    EntitySnapshot,\n    EntitySnapshotDiff,\n    RelationSnapshotEntry,\n    TraitRegistry,\n    WorldSnapshot,\n    WorldSnapshotDiff,\n} from \'./snapshot/snapshot\';\n```\n\nIf the feature is to live in `src/snapshot/index.ts` (barrel) + `src/snapshot/snapshot.ts` (impl), the only change needed is `from \'./snapshot\'` — the barrel then re-exports from `./snapshot`.\n\n---\n\n## (b) `packages/core/src/world/world.ts` — World shape, public methods, JSDoc/typing conventions\n\n### b.1 Shape\n\n`World` is a **plain object literal** built by `createWorld()`, not a class. It is typed by the `World` interface in `packages/core/src/world/types.ts`. Internal state hangs off the `$internal` symbol.\n\n`packages/core/src/world/types.ts:1-20` (imports + interface head)\n\n```ts\nimport { ActionInstance } from \'../actions/types\';\nimport type { $internal } from \'../common\';\nimport type { Entity } from \'../entity/types\';\nimport type { createEntityIndex } from \'../entity/utils/entity-index\';\nimport type { Query, QueryInstance, QueryParameter, QueryResult, QueryUnsubscriber } from \'../query/types\';\nimport type { Relation, RelationPair } from \'../relation/types\';\n...\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/world/types.ts:55-71` — the public method surface (note: **no JSDoc on individual methods**; the interface is a bare signature list):\n\n```ts\n    init(...traits: ConfigurableTrait[]): void;\n    spawn(...traits: ConfigurableTrait[]): Entity;\n    has(entity: Entity): boolean;\n    has(trait: Trait): boolean;\n    has(target: Entity | Trait): boolean;\n    add(...traits: ConfigurableTrait[]): void;\n    remove(...traits: Trait[]): void;\n    get<T extends Trait>(trait: T): TraitRecord<ExtractSchema<T>> | undefined;\n    set<T extends Trait>(trait: T, value: TraitValue<ExtractSchema<T>> | SetTraitCallback<T>): void;\n    destroy(): void;\n    reset(): void;\n    snapshot(registry: TraitRegistry): WorldSnapshot;\n    rollback(registry: TraitRegistry, checkpoint: WorldSnapshot): void;\n    query<T extends QueryParameter[]>(key: Query<T>): QueryResult<T>;\n    query<T extends QueryParameter[]>(...parameters: T): QueryResult<T>;\n    queryFirst<T extends QueryParameter[]>(key: Query<T>): Entity | undefined;\n    queryFirst<T extends QueryParameter[]>(...parameters: T): Entity | undefined;\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/world/types.ts:13` — the draft\'s type-only import:\n\n```ts\nimport type { TraitRegistry, WorldSnapshot } from \'../snapshot/snapshot\';\n```\n\n### b.2 Implementation conventions\n\n`packages/core/src/world/world.ts:12-13` — value + type imports kept on separate lines:\n\n```ts\nimport { rollbackWorld, snapshotWorld } from \'../snapshot/snapshot\';\nimport type { TraitRegistry, WorldSnapshot } from \'../snapshot/snapshot\';\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/world/world.ts:180-193` — methods are **object-literal shorthand**, one-liners delegate to a free function, and are separated by blank lines:\n\n```ts\n            ctx.worldEntity = createEntity(world, IsExcluded);\n\n            for (const sub of ctx.resetSubscriptions) {\n                sub(world);\n            }\n        },\n\n        snapshot(registry: TraitRegistry): WorldSnapshot {\n            return snapshotWorld(world, registry);\n        },\n\n        rollback(registry: TraitRegistry, checkpoint: WorldSnapshot) {\n            return rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint);\n        },\n\n        query(...args: any[]) {\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/world/world.ts:376-380` — read-only properties are installed with `Object.defineProperty` getters (the pattern to follow if a snapshot API needs a non-method property):\n\n```ts\n    // Read-only properties via getters\n    Object.defineProperty(world, \'i...\n```\n\n**Conventions to imitate for new World methods:**\n\n1. Add the signature to the `World` interface in `world/types.ts` (no JSDoc there — the file has none).\n2. Add a shorthand method in the `createWorld` object literal in `world.ts`, delegating to a free function in the feature module.\n3. Import the free function as a **value** import and the types as a **separate `import type`** line.\n4. Keep the method body a single `return freeFunction(world, ...)`.\n\n---\n\n## (c) `packages/core/src/entity/entity-methods-patch.ts` — exact attachment pattern\n\nEntities are **`number` primitives** (`packages/core/src/entity/types.ts:12`: `export type Entity = number & { ... }`). Convenience methods are therefore monkey-patched onto **`Number.prototype`**, guarded by `//@ts-expect-error`.\n\n`packages/core/src/entity/entity-methods-patch.ts:1-20` (head)\n\n```ts\n// Add methods to the Number prototype so it can be used as an entity.\n// This lets us...\nimport { $internal } from \'../common\';\nimport type { Entity } from \'../entity/types\';\nimport { getEntityGeneration, getEntityId } from \'./utils/pack-entity\';\n\n// @ts-expect-error\n```\n\n`packages/core/src/entity/entity-methods-patch.ts:85-101` — the **exact pattern to copy** for new per-entity methods:\n\n```ts\n    const entityIndex = world[$internal].entityIndex;\n    return isEntityAlive(entityIndex, this);\n};\n\n//@ts-expect-error\nNumber.prototype.snapshot = function (this: Entity, registry: TraitRegistry) {\n    return snapshotEntity(getEntityWorld(this), this, registry);\n};\n\n//@ts-expect-error\nNumber.prototype.rollback = function (\n    this: Entity,\n    registry: TraitRegistry,\n    snapshot: EntitySnapshot\n) {\n    return rollbackEntity(getEntityWorld(this), this, registry, snapshot);\n};\n```\n\nPattern rules:\n\n1. `//@ts-expect-error` on the line **immediately above** the assignment (note: no space after `//` in the draft, while the file head uses `// @ts-expect-error` — both forms occur; the draft\'s `//@ts-expect-error` matches the majority of the file).\n2. `Number.prototype.<name> = function (this: Entity, ...args) { ... }` — `this` is explicitly typed as `Entity`.\n3. The body resolves the owning world via `getEntityWorld(this)` and delegates to a free function.\n4. The corresponding signature must be added to the `Entity` intersection type in `packages/core/src/entity/types.ts:12-31`:\n\n```ts\nexport type Entity = number & {\n    add: (...traits: ConfigurableTrait[]) => void;\n    remove: (...traits: (Trait | RelationPair)[]) => void;\n    has: (trait: Trait | RelationPair) => boolean;\n    destroy: () => void;\n    changed: (trait: Trait) => void;\n    set: <T extends Trait | RelationPair>(\n        trait: T,\n        value: TraitValue<ExtractSchema<T>> | SetTraitCallback<T>,\n        flagChanged?: boolean\n    ) => void;\n    get: <T extends Trait | RelationPair>(trait: T) => TraitRecord<ExtractSchema<T>> | undefined;\n    targetFor: <T extends Trait>(relation: Relation<T>) => Entity | undefined;\n    targetsFor: <T extends Trait>(relation: Relation<T>) => Entity[];\n    id: () => number;\n    generation: () => number;\n    isAlive: () => boolean;\n    snapshot: (registry: TraitRegistry) => EntitySnapshot;\n    rollback: (registry: TraitRegistry, snapshot: EntitySnapshot) => void;\n};\n```\n\nwith the type-only import at `packages/core/src/entity/types.ts:2`:\n\n```ts\nimport type { EntitySnapshot, TraitRegistry } from \'../snapshot/snapshot\';\n```\n\n---\n\n## (e) Entity creation / destruction and id recycling\n\n### e.1 Ids are **recycled** — this is the single most important rollback constraint\n\n`packages/core/src/entity/utils/entity-index.ts` maintains a **sparse-set** (`sparse`, `dense`, `aliveCount`, `maxId`). The draft\'s `resolveTarget` (`snapshot.ts:261-271`) and `ensureEntityWithId` (`snapshot.ts:380-408`) both read that structure directly:\n\n```ts\nfunction resolveTarget(world: World, targetId: number): Entity {\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n    const sparse = ctx.entityIndex.sparse;\n    const dense = ctx.entityIndex.dense;\n    const denseIndex = sparse[targetId];\n    if (denseIndex !== undefined && denseIndex < ctx.entityIndex.aliveCount) {\n        const candidate = dense[denseIndex];\n        if (getEntityId(candidate) === targetId) return candidate;\n    }\n    throw new Error(`Koota: Relation target entity ${targetId} does not exist in the world.`);\n}\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction ensureEntityWithId(world: World, id: number): Entity {\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n    const index = ctx.entityIndex;\n    const sparse = index.sparse;\n    const dense = index.dense;\n    const denseIndex = sparse[id];\n\n    if (denseIndex !== undefined && denseIndex < index.aliveCount) {\n        const candidate = dense[denseIndex];\n        if (getEntityId(candidate) === id) return candidate;\n    }\n\n    const entity = packEntity(index.worldId, 0, id);\n\n    // Grow the dense array if needed.\n    while (dense.length <= index.aliveCount) dense.push(0 as Entity);\n    dense[index.aliveCount] = entity;\n    sparse[id] = index.aliveCount;\n    index.aliveCount++;\n    if (id >= index.maxId) index.maxId = id + 1;\n\n    ctx.entityTraits.set(entity, new Set());\n\n    // Keep the all-query in sync.\n    const allQuery = ctx.queriesHashMap.get(\'\');\n    if (allQuery) allQuery.add(entity);\n\n    return entity;\n}\n```\n\n**Facts:**\n\n- An `Entity` is a **packed number**: `packEntity(worldId, generation, id)` (`entity/utils/pack-entity.ts`), unpacked with `getEntityId` / `getEntityGeneration`.\n- The **id** is the sparse-set key; the **generation** disambiguates a recycled id from a stale handle.\n- `isEntityAlive(entityIndex, entity)` (`entity/utils/entity-index.ts`) is the liveness check; the draft wraps it as `assertAlive` (`snapshot.ts:178-182`):\n\n```ts\nfunction assertAlive(world: World, entity: Entity) {\n    if (!isEntityAlive(world[$internal].entityIndex, entity)) {\n        throw new Error(\'Koota: The entity does not exist.\');\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- **Rollback of destroyed entities:** because ids are recycled, a snapshot keyed by `id` alone can be *wrong* if the id was reused by a different entity between snapshot and rollback. The draft\'s `ensureEntityWithId` **recreates with generation `0`** (`packEntity(index.worldId, 0, id)`), which means a rolled-back entity is a *different handle* from the original if the original had a non-zero generation. Any test that holds an old `Entity` handle across a `rollbackWorld` will see `isAlive() === false` for that handle. This is a **design decision that must be documented** (or changed to preserve the original generation, which requires storing `generation` in `EntitySnapshot` — the draft does **not**).\n- `EntitySnapshot` currently stores only `id: number` (`snapshot.ts:26-30`), **not** generation. That is the gap to close if generation-preserving rollback is required.\n\n### e.2 Destruction\n\n`packages/core/src/entity/entity.ts` exports `destroyEntity(world, entity)` (imported at `snapshot.ts:2`). `rollbackWorld` destroys everything except the world entity first, then recreates:\n\n`packages/core/src/snapshot/snapshot.ts:442-459`\n\n```ts\n    const ctx = world[$internal];\n\n    // Destroy every entity except the world entity.\n    for (const entity of world.entities) {\n        if (entity === ctx.worldEntity) continue;\n        if (world.has(entity)) destroyEntity(world, entity);\n    }\n\n    // Recreate entities with the same IDs.\n    const recreated = new Map<number, Entity>();\n    for (const snapshot of checkpoint.entities) {\n        recreated.set(snapshot.id, ensureEntityWithId(world, snapshot.id));\n    }\n\n    // Apply traits and relations now that every target entity exists.\n    for (const snapshot of checkpoint.entities) {\n        applyEntitySnapshot(world, recreated.get(snapshot.id)!, registry, snapshot);\n    }\n```\n\n- The **world entity** (`ctx.worldEntity`, created with `IsExcluded` at `world.ts:180`) must be excluded from snapshots and from destruction — the draft does both (`snapshot.ts:253-255` and `snapshot.ts:445-448`).\n- `world.entities` is the iteration source for `snapshotWorld` (`snapshot.ts:253`).\n\n---\n\n## (f) `package.json` scripts and test layout / import conventions\n\n### f.1 `packages/core/package.json`\n\n```json\n{\n  "name": "@koota/core",\n  "version": "0.0.1",\n  "description": "Core vanilla library for Koota",\n  ...\n  "scripts": {\n    "test": "vitest",\n    "lint": "oxlint"\n  },\n  "devDependencies": {\n    "@config/oxlint": "workspace:*",\n    "@config/typescript": "workspace:*",\n    "vitest": "catalog:"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- **Test runner: `vitest`** (`pnpm -F core test run` from the root, per root `package.json`).\n- **Linter: `oxlint`** with shared config `@config/oxlint`.\n- **TypeScript config: `@config/typescript`** (shared, in `.config/typescript/`).\n- No `exports` map, no build step in this package — it is consumed as source by the workspace.\n\n### f.2 Test layout\n\n```\npackages/core/tests/\n├── actions.test.ts\n├── entity.test.ts\n├── ordered.test.ts\n├── query-modifiers.test.ts\n├── query.test.ts\n├── relation.test.ts\n├── trait.test.ts\n├── world.test.ts\n└── utils/\n    └── sparse-set.test.ts\n```\n\n- **Flat `tests/` directory**, one file per feature area, named `<area>.test.ts`. A new feature gets `tests/snapshot.test.ts`.\n- **Import convention** — `tests/trait.test.ts:1-2`:\n\n```ts\nimport { beforeEach, describe, expect, it, vi } from \'vitest\';\nimport { createWorld, type Entity, getStore, trait } from \'../src\';\n```\n\n  i.e. tests import from **`\'../src\'`** (the package barrel), never from deep paths, and use **inline `type` modifiers** (`type Entity`) rather than a separate `import type` line. `beforeEach`/`describe`/`expect`/`it`/`vi` are the standard vitest imports.\n- `tests/query-modifiers.test.ts:754` contains a comment mentioning "snapshots entityMasks" — that is about the `Changed` modifier\'s internal mask snapshot, **unrelated** to this feature; no existing test exercises the new API.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed public API signatures\n\nThese match the in-tree draft (`src/snapshot/snapshot.ts`) and the conventions above. Types are listed first, then functions, then the convenience methods.\n\n### Types\n\n```ts\n/** A single relation target entry in an entity snapshot. */\nexport type RelationSnapshotEntry = {\n    targetId: number;\n    data?: object;\n};\n\n/** A snapshot of a single entity\'s traits and relations. */\nexport type EntitySnapshot = {\n    id: number;\n    traits: Record<string, object | true>;          // true === tag trait present\n    relations?: Record<string, RelationSnapshotEntry[]>;\n};\n\n/** A snapshot of an entire world. */\nexport type WorldSnapshot = {\n    entities: EntitySnapshot[];\n};\n\n/** Result of diffing two entity snapshots. */\nexport type EntitySnapshotDiff = {\n    addedTraits: string[];\n    removedTraits: string[];\n    changedTraits: string[];\n};\n\n/** Result of diffing two world snapshots. */\nexport type WorldSnapshotDiff = {\n    added: number[];\n    removed: number[];\n    changed: number[];\n};\n\ntype RegistryEntry = {\n    key: string;\n    trait: Trait;\n    relation: Relation<Trait> | null;\n};\n\n/**\n * A registry of traits and relations keyed by name. Snapshots and rollbacks\n * use the registry to translate between stable string keys and trait/relation\n * definitions.\n */\nexport type TraitRegistry = {\n    readonly entries: ReadonlyMap<string, RegistryEntry>;\n    get(key: string): Trait | Relation<Trait> | undefined;\n    has(key: string): boolean;\n    keys(): string[];\n};\n```\n\n### Functions\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Create a registry from `[key, Trait | Relation]` entries.\n * Throws on duplicate keys, duplicate traits, or duplicate relations.\n */\nexport function createTraitRegistry(\n    ...entries: Array<[string, Trait | Relation<Trait>]>\n): TraitRegistry;\n\n/**\n * Snapshot a single entity\'s traits and relations.\n */\nexport function snapshotEntity(\n    world: World,\n    entity: Entity,\n    registry: TraitRegistry\n): EntitySnapshot;\n\n/**\n * Snapshot every entity in the world,' additional_kwargs={'refusal': None} response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 8192, 'prompt_tokens': 48774, 'total_tokens': 56966, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': {'audio_tokens': None, 'cached_tokens': 48640}, 'prompt_cache_hit_tokens': 48640, 'prompt_cache_miss_tokens': 134}, 'model_provider': 'deepseek', 'model_name': 'deepseek-flash', 'system_fingerprint': 'bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6', 'id': '3a6f3141-89cc-4167-bbf7-0321c0c60172', 'finish_reason': 'length', 'logprobs': None} id='lc_run--01a213e1-342c-7b81-a478-6e7a0fcfd6bd-0' tool_calls=[] invalid_tool_calls=[] usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 48774, 'output_tokens': 8192, 'total_tokens': 56966, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 48640}, 'output_token_details': {}}
